**Read:** [Static vacuum insulated cryogenic vessels — EIGA 224/20](../sources/Static-Cryogenic-Vessels-EIGA.lingua#r_3RWNED7WZ5GMG7X76ZT96A63HZ), equipment, insulation and inspection sections; [Vacuum systems — Vincent Baglin and Roberto Kersevan](../sources/Vacuum-Systems-Baglin-Kersevan.lingua#r_1K9ATEHFM7ET2H8TRHC3QNCRFN), residual gas and cryogenic sections.

Follow the inner product vessel, the evacuated insulation space, outer jacket, supports and penetrations. Each has a different function and boundary condition. A gauge measuring product pressure says nothing by itself about the state of the insulation vacuum. Conversely, good insulation vacuum does not establish product composition or delivery capacity.

Heat can enter by solid supports and piping, radiation, residual gas and imperfections in insulation. Product withdrawal, vapor return and pressure-building circuits redistribute mass and energy. The same physical vessel therefore participates in both a gas-delivery problem and a vacuum-maintenance problem.

Use the earlier molecular heat-transfer and outgassing Records to explain why insulation performance can change over time. Keep the actual insulation architecture and operating temperatures in view; a simple empty-gap radiation calculation is not a complete multilayer-insulation model.

**Work:** Draw a section through a conceptual vessel and assign every heat path to a mechanism. For each observation—outer-surface temperature, vacuum indication, product pressure or loss of inventory—list more than one possible cause before selecting a diagnostic measurement.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
